-- title:
--   p. 247 — $\Pi$ is isomorphic to $\Sigma$
-- statement:
--   The subgroup $\Pi$ of $V_1$ generated by $\pi_0, \pi_1, \pi_2, \dots$ is isomorphic to $\Sigma$, the group of permutations of $\mathbf{N}$ with finite support, by an isomorphism sending each $\pi_i$ to the transposition $s_i$ of $i$ and $i + 1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source defines $\Pi = \bigcup_{n} \Pi(n)$; since the $\Pi(n)$ increase, this union is the subgroup generated by all the $\pi_i$, which is how $\Pi$ is taken here.
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) 42 (1996) 215–256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, section 6, p. 247, before Lemma 6.7

